W44-180 incremental-histogram DC tree split scan — paired A/B/cjxl bench
========================================================================

Date:      2026-05-22
Commit:    pending (see git log -- jxl-encoder/src/vardct/dc_tree_learn.rs)
Host:      water-cooled AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 128 GB RAM (Lilith River workstation)
Build:     cargo run --release --features '__expert parallel butteraugli-loop ssim2-loop' \
             --example w44_180_inc_hist_ab
cjxl:      v0.12.0 4279d48 [_AVX3_,AVX2,SSE4,SSE2] {GNU 11.4.0}, default threads
Threads:   32 (default rayon pool for ours; cjxl default)
Time iters: 3 per cell, best-of-3 wall reported

What this measures
==================

Mode A (BEFORE, pre-W44-180): per-quantile re-scan via
  `JXL_W44_180_FORCE_LEGACY_DC_TREE_SPLIT=1` env var. Each of the 32
  quantile candidate splitvals re-partitions the indices and re-builds the
  per-side histograms from scratch (O(N · P) per side × 32 × 2 = 64
  O(N · P) scans per node).

Mode B (AFTER, W44-180 default): incremental-histogram update via libjxl
  `enc_ma.cc:280-439` pattern. Pre-compute parent totals once, then sweep
  candidate splitvals in ascending order while transferring buckets
  incrementally from `counts_above` → `counts_below`. Per-candidate cost
  evaluation is O(P · S) where S = max_token + 1.

cjxl: reference encoder at the same effort/distance, default threads.

