System load context
===================

Concurrent rustc/cargo builds from W44-178, W44-179, zenmetrics, and
zensim-gpu agents were active during this bench. Load avg ranged from
177 to 304 throughout. Both A and B modes (and cjxl) ran under the same
contention, so wall-time A/B ratios are still meaningful but the absolute
numbers (a_ms, b_ms, cjxl_ms) are ~5-30× slower than they would be on an
idle machine. Cross-comparison with the W44-175 baseline TSV
(`benchmarks/w44_175_parallel_buttloop_ab_2026-05-21.tsv`) is NOT
apples-to-apples for wall time — only the byte-identical and SSIM2-delta
columns are directly comparable.

Acceptance gates
================

The W44-180 task spec's PRIMARY acceptance criterion is byte-identical
output. The bench TSV confirms this on all 10 cells:

- bytes_identical_a_b == 1 on every row ✓
- ssim2_delta_b_minus_a = +0.000000 on every row ✓
- bfly_delta_b_minus_a  = +0.000000 on every row ✓

The wall ratio target (≤2.5× cjxl on top-3 e8 wedges) is NOT met under
this load. Even on an idle machine, the wall ratio cap would require
algorithmic improvements ELSEWHERE in the buttloop pipeline (transform,
quant, AC strategy search, EPF, etc.). W44-180 is scoped to just the DC
tree split-scan inner cost evaluation — that single function's wall
contribution is bounded by Amdahl on the parallelized W44-175 shape.

Notable wall-time results
=========================

- **terminal_e9_d05_PROTECT: 9.06× speedup (13.1s → 1.4s)** — e9 uses
  `PredictorSet::Variable` (14 predictors); the per-quantile re-scan
  legacy path was paying 14× the cost vs Best mode. This is the cleanest
  measurement in the harness because both A and B ran under the same
  system load and the e9 wall is dominated by the DC tree learning path
  (W44-172 measured ~47% CPU on this path pre-W44-172, ~22% post-W44-172).
- **terminal_e8_d1: 1.63× speedup (9.2s → 5.6s)** — e8 uses
  `PredictorSet::Best` (2 predictors).
- **imac_dark_e8_d05: 1.35× speedup (28.2s → 20.8s)**
- terminal_e8_d05: 1.05× (load noise, but byte-identical)
- codec_wiki_e8_d05: 0.98× (load noise, but byte-identical)
- PROTECT cells: byte-identical, noisy timing (system load dominates)

Why the wall-time delta varies across cells
===========================================

The DC tree learning runs ONCE per encode. With W44-175's 14-way per-
property parallel fan-out, the function's wall-time contribution is
roughly:

  wall_dc_tree = sum_over_props(per_property_serial_work) / parallelism

For a 14-prop parallel call on 14+ cores, the per-property serial work
dominates. The pre-W44-180 per-property work was O(N · P · Q · 2). The
W44-180 incremental update reduces this to O(N · P + Q · P · S), a
~30× algorithmic speedup on typical e9 inputs (N≈100k, P=14, Q=32, S≈30).

Net wall-time savings = saved_dc_tree_time × (1 - other_pipeline / total)

When the buttloop runs more iterations (e9 or higher distance), the DC
tree path is called more often per encode → larger wall fraction → larger
absolute wall savings. When the buttloop runs few iterations (e8 d=0.5
on a small screenshot), the DC tree path is a small fraction → savings
get lost in load noise.

Cross-comparison with W44-175 wall ratios (different machine state, less load)
=============================================================================

W44-175 measured wall_ratio_b_cjxl on an idler machine:
  terminal_e8_d05: 7.350
  codec_wiki_e8_d05: 9.185
  imac_dark_e8_d05: 10.991
  terminal_e9_d05_PROTECT: 15.225

W44-180 under heavy load measures:
  terminal_e8_d05: 4.983 (-32%)
  codec_wiki_e8_d05: 9.274 (~equal, load noise)
  imac_dark_e8_d05: 6.055 (-45%)
  terminal_e9_d05_PROTECT: 12.016 (-21%)

The improvements ARE real but the load-noise envelope is ~20-30% on
these timings. Hitting the 2.5× target requires additional algorithmic
work (W44-181+ scope per the W44-180 task memo).

DO NOT
======

- DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte movement (per W44-66 user correction).
- DO NOT regen the hash-lock fixtures based on this bench — they were already
  verified BYTE-IDENTICAL by `cargo test --test hash_lock_features` (36/36)
  and `cargo test --test strategy_libjxl_hash_locks` (5/5).
- DO NOT remove the `JXL_W44_180_FORCE_LEGACY_DC_TREE_SPLIT` env hook. It's
  the bisection lever for any future divergence A/B repro.
- DO NOT widen the candidate splitval set from 32-quantile to libjxl's
  "every distinct value". That would require hash-lock regen + re-measurement
  on every existing bench. W44-180 is scoped to the inner cost-evaluation
  algorithm ONLY.
